# Scoped equivalents of xtask/src/trust_compiler_observer.rs, exact native text pairs.
# Source/API/caller proof and controls: book Annex C, explicit-close assessment.

from-json "drop(file);"
to-json "// drop(file);"
why In the current private Unix observer protocol, file is an exclusively created unbuffered std::fs::File, fully written and successfully sync_all-checked before publication, with no locks, clones, later writes or ownership transfer. Omitting this explicit drop only defers the same ignored-error destructor until observe returns, including either publication ? error; main exits afterward. The intervening path-only hard_link and remove_file preserve a completed hard link and use no descriptor allocation or close-dependent state. The sole production reader calls Session::invocations only after synchronous successful Cargo completion, by which time either variant has dropped the File. Required record bytes, no-replacement publication, partial evidence, diagnostics and exit/signal results are unchanged under these ordinary Unix regular-file semantics. This does not claim equal descriptor lifetime, syscall timing, crash/resource schedules or arbitrary close-sensitive filesystems; a native trace deliberately distinguishes close timing. Source/caller hashes, pinned query, actual omission, independent protocol/error expectations and wider timing/wrong-oracle controls are held outside the semantic parent population in ObserverCloseTests. Changed writer type, locking/buffering, post-sync operations, producer, caller, concurrent readers or platform semantics require revalidation (leaf M3.6.9.6.3.2.2.15.2.1.3.3.3.2.3).
